import BSCAveraging.CoreDeriv

/-! # Regime 2 of (iii)'s core, by a certified sweep

`NOTES.md` §7f⁹.  The middle regime `[0.45, 3]` of the one-variable core of
(iii) is discharged here by the centred sweep of `CoreDeriv.lean`: 88 cells,
each carrying its range, the `log` witnesses valid over the cell (for the
Lipschitz bound on `F′`) and at its centre (for the point enclosure of `F`).

Every cell is checked by `cellOkC`, which verifies in exact rational arithmetic
that `L·(b−a) < F(centre)` with `L` an enclosure-derived bound on `|F′|`; the
tiling is checked by `tilesC`.  `sweepC_sound` then gives `F > 0` on the whole
interval.

The checks run in the **kernel** (`decide +kernel`), not through
`native_decide`: this file adds no axiom at all.  Three things make that
affordable.  The series parameters are cut to the cheapest ones that still pass,
`n = 10` terms and rounding grid `m = 10⁹` (the generated witnesses have ample
slack — even `n = 8` passes).  The widths are adaptive: the generator takes at
each step the widest cell on the `1/1000` grid that still passes the test, which
is what brings the count down from the 650 of a near-uniform tiling to 88 — the
margin `F(centre) − L·(b−a)` varies by two orders of magnitude over `[0.45, 3]`,
so uniform widths overspend everywhere except at the left end.  And the cells
are checked in blocks of ten: kernel reduction retains every intermediate `ℚ`,
so one `decide` over the whole list needs tens of gigabytes, while the blocks
plateau at about four.  Cost: about 45 seconds (it was six minutes at 650
cells), against fourteen seconds for `native_decide`.

The witnesses bracket the *enclosure* of `log(cosh 2θ)`, not its true range:
`coshI` forms its lower bound as `(e^{2a} + e^{−2b})/2`, pairing the two
anti-correlated terms at opposite endpoints, so the enclosure is genuinely wider
than the true range and witnesses taken from the latter fail.

Generated by `numerics/p0_gen_cells.py`. -/

set_option maxRecDepth 100000
set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000

namespace BSCAveraging.Core


